+    surfaces learned that the hard way, and both were fixed one source at a time:
+
+    - hosted rooms (``source=bot_room``) held a member's slot for the life of a live session no
+      reaper ever ends, locking every other room worker sharing the home — messaging gateway and
+      Desktop ``serve`` — out of that member until this process exited (#106847);
+    - the Desktop held a chat's slot for as long as the chat stayed open. Measured on the owner's
+      machine: three leases written 9.0, 7.3 and 6.8 hours earlier and never touched since, one per
+      chat the app had open and was not using.
+
+    Keying the release on the session's SOURCE was the remaining bug. A Bot Chat's stored source is
+    ``cli``, so the Desktop's lease on it was never released and the phone was refused — "session
+    20260930_222435_469712 opened by desktop 1h35m ago" when the owner tried to chat with that bot
+    from the bots tab. The question is the same for every client: is this turn still in flight? So
+    the release no longer asks who it is.
+
+    Releasing at the end of the turn keeps the property the fence exists for — never two writers
+    inside one turn — and lets the NEXT turn be taken by whichever device the user picks up.
+    ``_admit_prompt_turn`` → ``_ensure_active_session_slot`` re-claims on the following turn, so a
+    turn never runs lease-less. Call under ``history_lock`` next to ``running = False`` so the next
+    admission never observes the stale lease and then runs lease-less.
     """
